WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:11.579
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel, and my name is Paul. Today's topic is the Amharic language, or

00:00:12.098 --> 00:00:17.308
"Amarennya" as it's known in Amharic. Amharic is one of the major languages of

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:22.079
Ethiopia, and it's the official working language of the Ethiopian federal government.

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:30.149
Regional and local authorities are free to choose their working language in Ethiopia but Amharic is the working language of several regions. According to the

00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:38.489
2007 census, it's the native language of 22 million people, or around 30% of the population of 74 million.

00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:43.438
But over the last 10 years the population of Ethiopia Is thought to have surpassed

00:00:43.839 --> 00:00:48.748
100 million and the number of native Amharic speakers is probably over 30 million.

00:00:49.029 --> 00:00:55.438
But Ethiopia is a country where many people are multilingual, and millions more people speak Amharic as a second language,

00:00:55.538 --> 00:00:57.538
especially in cities and towns.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:02.008
Amharic is a member of the Semitic language family, which

00:01:02.009 --> 00:01:08.849
also includes Hebrew, Arabic, Aramaic, and the Tigre and Tigrinya languages, which are spoken in Ethiopia and Eritrea.

00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:14.008
All Semitic languages developed from the theoretic Proto-Semitic language.

00:01:14.469 --> 00:01:20.818
The Ethiopic, or Ethio-Semitic, languages are part of the South Semitic branch, and the most common theory

00:01:20.818 --> 00:01:27.088
is that all Ethiosemitic languages, including Amharic, developed from a common Proto-Ethiosemitic language.

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:33.358
This Proto-Ethiosemitic Language is thought to have developed from the language of migrants from southern Arabia,

00:01:33.848 --> 00:01:38.758
presumably Old South Arabian languages. These migrants intermixed with the native people,

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:43.739
who spoke Cushitic languages, and Proto-Ethiosemitic arose. This language

00:01:43.739 --> 00:01:47.559
was a direct descendant of the language of the migrants. From this

00:01:47.559 --> 00:01:55.039
Proto-Ethiosemitic language, two branches developed: the Southern branch, which includes Amharic, and the Northern branch, which includes Tigre,

00:01:55.239 --> 00:02:03.028
Tigrinya, and Ge'ez, the oldest attested Ethio-Semitic language, and the one that became the official language of the Aksumite Empire and

00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:07.019
remained the official literary language in Ethiopia until the 19th century.

00:02:08.019 --> 00:02:12.478
Some believe in that theory of migration from southern Arabia, but some believe that

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:17.727
Amharic developed from a Semitic language that had existed in the area before that migration.

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:22.259
And in recent years, another theory has arisen: that Amharic is actually a

00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:26.179
creole that arose from contact between Semitic-speaking officers and

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:32.158
Cushitic-speaking soldiers in the Aksumite Empire after the 4th century. But this theory is hotly contested

00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:37.499
and is not the general consensus. The earliest known Amharic writing is from the 13th Century,

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:40.879
even though it was mainly a spoken language at that time.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:47.459
This was the time of the Solomonic dynasty, when King Yakuno 'Amlak made Amharic the spoken lingua franca of the

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:52.498
Ethiopian court, and it became known as the language of the king - "leshana negus".

00:02:53.229 --> 00:02:59.998
Ge'ez, the language of the previous Aksumite rulers, continued to be used as the language of literature - "leshana sehuf".

00:03:00.458 --> 00:03:04.138
This resulted in a diglossic situation that would last for centuries,

00:03:04.419 --> 00:03:08.909
with Ge'ez being used for literature and in the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, and

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:13.378
Amharic being used in speech. In the 15th century, under the rule of Zara Yaqob,

00:03:13.928 --> 00:03:21.118
troops who collected taxes for the king likely brought Amharic into other regions further south. In the mid-19th century,

00:03:21.669 --> 00:03:24.389
Amharic began to replace Ge'ez as the literary language.

00:03:24.789 --> 00:03:32.608
Case in point: the Ethiopian emperors Tewodros II and Menelik II had their chronicles written in Amharic rather than Ge'ez,

00:03:32.708 --> 00:03:37.618
which had been used for previous emperors. Under Menelik II, Ethiopia grew

00:03:37.618 --> 00:03:44.379
into something resembling its current borders. Amharic became the de facto official language, and Amharic-speaking officials

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:46.539
were responsible for the newly incorporated

00:03:46.539 --> 00:03:50.599
territories. The spread of the printing press in the 19th and 20th centuries

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:57.178
contributed further to the spread of Amharic as, in the 20th century, most new print materials, including the first newspaper,

00:03:57.279 --> 00:03:59.519
were written in Amharic rather than Ge'ez

00:04:00.149 --> 00:04:01.839
In the early 20th century,

00:04:01.839 --> 00:04:03.059
Italian, French, and English

00:04:03.059 --> 00:04:07.588
were used as the main languages of school instruction, but then, in 1944,

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:13.319
Amharic became the sole language of instruction in primary schools, under the rule of Haile Selassie.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:18.339
He then made Amharic the official language of Ethiopia in 1955.

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:21.499
During the socialist government known as "the Derg", from

00:04:21.659 --> 00:04:29.159
1974 to 1991, increased development, urbanization, and an increase in the number of public schools and adult literacy

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:33.268
programs caused Amharic to spread even more. But still, Amharic

00:04:33.268 --> 00:04:40.258
was much more widely spoken in towns and cities, and not as widely spoken in rural areas, except in its original native regions.

00:04:40.538 --> 00:04:45.928
After 1991, language policies were changed to give more influence to the other languages of

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.659
Ethiopia, and there are over 80 of them. Currently,

00:04:49.059 --> 00:04:54.958
regional and local authorities can choose their own working language, and their own language of primary school education.

00:04:55.089 --> 00:05:00.418
But Amharic remains the most widely spoken lingua franca, with a large proportion of the urban population

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:08.428
throughout the country being able to speak Amharic. So what is Amharic like? First of all, what's this interesting script that Amharic is written in?

00:05:08.559 --> 00:05:13.618
Well, that's actually the Ge'ez script, which is used not only to write the Ge'ez language, but

00:05:13.619 --> 00:05:19.858
also Amharic, Tigrinya, and others. The Ge'ez script is an abugida - each character represents a syllable, and

00:05:20.018 --> 00:05:26.577
consists of a consonant as its major component, plus an attached ligature or other modification that represents a vowel.

00:05:26.577 --> 00:05:30.358
In Amharic, this Ge'ez-based writing system is called "fidal".

00:05:30.879 --> 00:05:37.479
In Amharic, there are plain consonants, similar to most of the consonants in English, and there are glottalized consonants,

00:05:37.479 --> 00:05:42.088
(or "ejective consonants"). Glottalized consonants are produced by closing and reopening

00:05:42.309 --> 00:05:46.979
the glottis - the space between your vocal cords - while making the consonant sound.

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:52.678
When transliterated using Latin characters, they usually have a dot underneath them to distinguish them from their

00:05:52.679 --> 00:05:58.179
non-glottalized equivalents. Let's compare a plain consonant with its glottalized equivalent.

00:06:02.579 --> 00:06:04.719
Also, notice the doubled consonant.

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:13.619
All consonants can be short or long, long meaning that the consonant is doubled - double in length. The distinction between short

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:20.309
and long consonants is important, because sometimes the meaning of words is distinguished by the length of the consonant. For example:

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:22.768
"ala" - this means "He said."

00:06:22.879 --> 00:06:25.439
"alla" - this means "There is."

00:06:25.779 --> 00:06:28.179
"wana" - this means "swimming".

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:30.611
"wanna" - this means "important".

00:06:30.611 --> 00:06:32.968
So in both cases the length of the consonant

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:40.379
distinguishes the meaning of the words. Doubled consonants are not distinguished in writing, but in context Amharic speakers have little trouble with that.

00:06:41.168 --> 00:06:42.167
Sentence Structure

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:43.888
First, let's look at an equational

00:06:43.888 --> 00:06:45.888
sentence using the verb "to be":

00:06:46.059 --> 00:06:53.428
"legu acher naw" - this means "The boy is short." Word for word, it's "boy-the short is".

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:59.129
"-u" is a definite article. "leg" is "boy". "legu" is "the boy".

00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:03.658
Notice that the verb "to be" is at the end, and the adjective comes before it.

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:12.879
Amharic is a verb-final language, so in predicative sentences the word order is SV or OV or SOV.

00:07:12.879 --> 00:07:17.608
Of course, some sentences have no object, just a subject and an intransitive verb. For example,

00:07:18.189 --> 00:07:22.979
"legu waddaqa" - this sentence means "The child fell." Word for word, it's

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:29.279
"child-the he fell". So this is the subject, and this is the verb. And here's a sentence that does have a direct object:

00:07:29.728 --> 00:07:35.179
"addannu anbassa gaddala" - it means "The hunter killed a lion." Word for word, it's

00:07:35.179 --> 00:07:39.568
"hunter-the lion he killed". Here's the subject, the object, and the verb.

00:07:40.209 --> 00:07:47.919
Something we can add to this sentence is the optional direct object marker - "addannu anbassan gaddala". If you use the direct

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:53.578
object marker, then it's also possible to place the object at the beginning of the sentence, making it OSV.

00:07:53.779 --> 00:07:56.419
"anbassan addannu gaddala"

00:07:57.579 --> 00:08:03.379
And now let's try adding an adjective to this sentence: "addannu telleq anbassa gaddala".

00:08:03.548 --> 00:08:05.637
This sentence now means "The hunter killed

00:08:05.638 --> 00:08:09.027
a big lion." And you can see that the adjective is placed before the noun.

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:13.108
The previous sentences all have an explicit subject, but there can

00:08:13.108 --> 00:08:19.108
also be no explicit subject. For example, this sentence meaning "He opened the door." - "barrun kaffata"

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:26.879
Word for word, it's "door-the (direct object marker) he opened". No explicit subject is necessary here, because we

00:08:26.879 --> 00:08:35.379
know from the form of the verb that it refers to the third-person masculine singular "he". So this sentence is just OV, Object-Verb.

00:08:35.379 --> 00:08:36.898
And that brings us to verbs.

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:41.369
Similar to in other Semitic languages, the verb consists of a stem and

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:45.379
suffixes which indicate the person, gender, and number of the subject.

00:08:45.379 --> 00:08:49.168
Let's take a look at this example verb, meaning "to break." Here's the stem of the

00:08:49.169 --> 00:08:53.979
perfect form, and the suffixes placed after the stem tell us about the doer of the action.

00:09:04.679 --> 00:09:07.979
Now let's look at the imperfect form. Based on this stem:

00:09:18.219 --> 00:09:19.419
The Root System

00:09:19.419 --> 00:09:25.498
Let's take a look at the stems of the perfect form and the imperfect form again, and let's focus on these letters here.

00:09:26.289 --> 00:09:34.258
Similar to in other Semitic languages, Amharic uses a system of root letters and templates, into which the root letters are inserted. Roots consist of

00:09:34.359 --> 00:09:39.388
consonants - commonly 3, but it can also be 2, 4, or 5 - and they are placed

00:09:39.388 --> 00:09:44.218
into templates, consisting of a vowel pattern as well as some consonantal affixes.

00:09:45.039 --> 00:09:51.898
The root gives us the core meaning, and the template gives us grammatical information and more specific information about the meaning.

00:09:52.088 --> 00:09:54.879
Notice that with this verb, the second root letter is doubled,

00:09:54.879 --> 00:09:59.187
or geminated, in the perfect form. This is true for some verbs, but not for others.

00:09:59.768 --> 00:10:03.447
Those are a few of the important features of Amharic. Let's look at a few more

00:10:03.669 --> 00:10:07.109
example sentences, and see what else we find. Here's a sentence meaning

00:10:07.109 --> 00:10:08.399
"We must go today."

00:10:10.399 --> 00:10:11.179
Word for word, it's

00:10:11.189 --> 00:10:13.339
"today to go we must".

00:10:13.339 --> 00:10:19.579
In this sentence we see a modal verb meaning "must". Just like other verbs, this modal verb is conjugated for the subject, in this case,

00:10:19.588 --> 00:10:25.707
"we". The modal verb comes at the end, and before that, we see the main verb, meaning "to go" in its infinitive form.

00:10:26.379 --> 00:10:27.619
Another sentence.

00:10:29.099 --> 00:10:31.417
This means "Where can I find a doctor?"

00:10:32.759 --> 00:10:33.719
Word for word, it's

00:10:33.839 --> 00:10:36.199
"doctor where is found".

00:10:36.279 --> 00:10:38.899
The word "hakim" is the same as the Arabic word for

00:10:39.008 --> 00:10:42.748
"doctor", and I would guess that this is a loan word. This word means "where".

00:10:43.148 --> 00:10:48.898
Question words usually come directly before the verb, and you can see that the object of the verb comes first.

00:10:49.179 --> 00:10:51.898
This is an imperfect verb, with these root

00:10:52.059 --> 00:10:58.157
consonants. The affixes show that this is the third-person singular form. And on to the next sentence.

00:11:00.379 --> 00:11:08.399
This means "I've lost my car keys." Word for word, it's "of car my key have been lost to me".

00:11:08.479 --> 00:11:13.679
This pattern here, "of + a noun + another noun", is a typical way of showing possession or

00:11:13.959 --> 00:11:17.939
connection between two nouns. And this is a verb with these two root

00:11:17.939 --> 00:11:23.599
consonants, which mean "to lose". But this particular verb template makes it reflexive, or intransitive,

00:11:23.939 --> 00:11:28.018
so it means "to be lost". This is the conjunct form of the verb, which can

00:11:28.018 --> 00:11:31.918
be used like the present perfect in English, and this ending here means something like

00:11:32.169 --> 00:11:35.699
"happened to me", showing that the speaker is affected by the action.

00:11:36.069 --> 00:11:40.918
Notice that in this sentence, the word for key is singular. Plural can often be understood from the

00:11:41.139 --> 00:11:45.719
context, even if you use the singular form, but we can also use an explicit plural form.

00:11:49.759 --> 00:11:52.739
Again, this means "I've lost my car keys", but here you can

00:11:52.739 --> 00:11:55.619
see that a plural ending has been added to the word for "key".

00:11:55.778 --> 00:12:00.598
But it's not entirely necessary to use the plural form. You can use the singular form, and, in

00:12:00.699 --> 00:12:04.439
context, it should be clear whether you're talking about just one or many.

00:12:05.019 --> 00:12:06.793
And on to the next sentence.

00:12:08.659 --> 00:12:12.259
This means "We love Ethiopian cuisine." Word for word, it's

00:12:12.559 --> 00:12:14.959
"of Ethiopia food we love"

00:12:14.999 --> 00:12:17.319
Again, we see that pattern showing possession or

00:12:17.619 --> 00:12:19.889
relationship between two nouns. And here

00:12:19.889 --> 00:12:24.718
we see the imperfect form of the verb meaning "to love". Here's the stem, and here are the affixes

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:28.999
telling us that this is first-person plural - in other words, the "we" form.

00:12:29.259 --> 00:12:30.939
And one more sentence:

00:12:33.279 --> 00:12:35.439
This means "I didn't break the vase".

00:12:37.259 --> 00:12:38.559
Word for word, it's

00:12:38.559 --> 00:12:41.519
"vase the I broke (negative conjugation)".

00:12:41.519 --> 00:12:48.579
In Amharic, there's a special negative perfect form of verbs. The negative form is made by adding this prefix and this suffix to the

00:12:48.599 --> 00:12:54.659
regular perfect form. And here's the word for "vase", and here the definite article suffix. But since it's

00:12:54.759 --> 00:12:59.059
following another vowel, it's written as "w" rather than "u".

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:02.899
As a Semitic language, Amharic has some vocabulary that

00:13:03.059 --> 00:13:06.419
overlaps with other Semitic languages like Hebrew and Arabic.

00:13:06.419 --> 00:13:10.779
Some of these similar words are loan words from other languages like Arabic or Ge'ez,

00:13:10.989 --> 00:13:15.568
but some are cognates that developed from a common ancestor as the words in other Semitic

00:13:15.669 --> 00:13:20.038
languages. And sometimes it's hard to know if the word is a loan word or an original cognate.

00:13:20.379 --> 00:13:21.659
For example, the word

00:13:21.659 --> 00:13:25.279
we saw before which means break - "sabbara" - the same

00:13:25.279 --> 00:13:30.499
third-person perfect form in Hebrew would be "shavar". And in Hebrew sometimes the second root

00:13:30.499 --> 00:13:36.419
letter, like a "v" sound, is doubled and pronounced like a "b" sound, which makes the similarity even more obvious.

00:13:36.679 --> 00:13:41.939
And we also saw the word "to kill" - "gaddala". This is the third person perfect form.

00:13:42.119 --> 00:13:47.759
In Hebrew, it's "qatal", meaning "to slay," or "to kill". In Arabic, it's "qutila".

00:13:47.759 --> 00:13:54.679
The consonants here are not exactly the same, but I think there's a connection between the "g" sound in Amharic and the "k" or "q" sounds

00:13:54.779 --> 00:14:00.359
in the other languages, and I think there's a connection between the "d" and the "t" sounds in the other languages.

00:14:00.399 --> 00:14:07.299
Another example - the word for "bury": "qabbara". In Hebrew, it's "kavar", and in Arabic, "qabara".

00:14:07.899 --> 00:14:13.479
In Amharic, the verb meaning "to hear" is "samma", and this is the third-person perfect

00:14:13.499 --> 00:14:18.259
form meaning "he heard". In Hebrew, it's "shama'". In Arabic, it's "sama3".

00:14:18.259 --> 00:14:25.049
In Hebrew and Arabic, there's an extra root letter that was lost in the Amharic word, and it has mostly become silent in modern Hebrew.

00:14:25.049 --> 00:14:26.499
But it's still written.

00:14:26.499 --> 00:14:32.119
In Amharic, the word for "eye" is "'ayin". In Hebrew, it's "'ayin". In Arabic, it's "3ayin".

00:14:32.599 --> 00:14:37.359
I would love to be able to say that the English word "eye" is related to those, but it's not.

00:14:38.079 --> 00:14:39.659
Another one: in Amharic, there's

00:14:39.659 --> 00:14:46.459
"bet", which means "house". In Hebrew, it's "bayit", but when it's used in a compound word with another noun it's pronounced "beit",

00:14:46.539 --> 00:14:51.279
like in "beit sefer", the word for school. In Arabic, it's "beit" or "bayit".

00:14:52.539 --> 00:14:57.779
But the amount of vocabulary that Amharic has in common with Hebrew and Arabic might be less than you'd expect.

00:14:58.089 --> 00:15:00.089
That's partly because a lot of the

00:15:00.099 --> 00:15:07.478
vocabulary is of Cushitic origin, as much as 30%, and other vocabulary is unique to Amharic or to Ethiosemitic.

00:15:09.079 --> 00:15:16.688
Amharic is a very interesting language, and it's a language with clearly Semitic features, including the verbal system and the root system using

00:15:16.849 --> 00:15:18.999
consonantal roots and inserting them into templates.

00:15:19.179 --> 00:15:24.728
Those are things I'm familiar with from other Semitic languages like Hebrew and Arabic. And that includes some of the vocabulary

00:15:24.739 --> 00:15:30.179
we just looked at a second ago. But some elements are quite different from other Semitic languages, such as its

00:15:30.179 --> 00:15:36.459
SOV word order. Amharic is a language that I would love to dig into more in the future at some point.

00:15:36.939 --> 00:15:40.939
In my last video, on Japanese, I forgot to ask a question of the day,

00:15:40.939 --> 00:15:44.219
so today I'm not going to forget. Here is the question of the day:

00:15:44.359 --> 00:15:49.599
For speakers of Amharic: How widely is Amharic spoken in your area and your community?

00:15:49.599 --> 00:15:53.179
And if you're an immigrant from Ethiopia living somewhere else in the world,

00:15:53.179 --> 00:15:56.499
what language do members of your community normally speak with each other?

00:15:56.879 --> 00:16:02.099
And for speakers of other Semitic languages: Have you noticed the similarities between Amharic and your language?

00:16:02.379 --> 00:16:04.599
What sorts of similarities have you noticed?

00:16:04.619 --> 00:16:08.219
Be sure to follow LangFocus on Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram.

00:16:08.219 --> 00:16:10.899
And once again I want to say thank you to all of my

00:16:10.899 --> 00:16:15.053
Patreon supporters, especially these wonderful people right here on the screen,

00:16:15.059 --> 00:16:17.679
for their monthly pledges. Thank you for watching

00:16:17.679 --> 00:16:19.499
and have a nice day.

